WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.290
MAX SALTONSTALL: Building
an infrastructure

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:03.660
has more options than ever,
but managing deployments

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.240
with automated systems
and doing it securely?

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.100
Join us as we talk with
Two Sigma about how

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.462
they made it happen on
this episode of Stack Chat.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.668
Thanks for joining us, Nick.

00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:16.210
Tell us a little
more about Two Sigma

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:17.935
and how you manage
your deployments.

00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:19.893
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: Sure,
thanks for having us.

00:00:19.893 --> 00:00:22.480
At Two Sigma we combine data
science and technology, as well

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.190
as financial
expertise, and we use

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.710
that to develop
sophisticated trading models.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:29.980
These help us uncover value
in the world's markets.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.229
As you can imagine, this
requires the right balance

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.510
of allowing multiple
stakeholders and researchers

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.870
to iterate on research pretty
rapidly, while still protecting

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.280
authorization and
controlling our IP.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.530
MAX SALTONSTALL: Can you
show us what that looks like?

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:42.970
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS:
Sure, so this

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:45.995
isn't an exact representation
of our specific environment,

00:00:45.995 --> 00:00:48.370
but it's a good abstraction
that we can use as a baseline

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.200
to talk about from.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.060
So we started out with just
some Super Admin identities,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.750
and then we decided to extend
our on-premise existing

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.390
source of truth for identity,
which in our case is LDAP,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.300
to Google Identity, and we
used Google Cloud Directory

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.620
Sync to do that.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.230
Then we use identity and
access management policies

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.970
to restrict who can access
Google Cloud resources.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.140
This includes things like
Google Cloud storage buckets,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.880
compute instances, other
services, BigQuery,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.020
a few others.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.090
MAX SALTONSTALL: How do you
lock down the permissions

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.470
for these new resources?

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.940
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: We create
a pretty granular identity

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.100
in access management
policy so we can limit

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.080
who has access to resources.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.610
And we also heavily
restrict things

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.540
that we consider
privileged operations.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.990
So for us, that's anything
that could impact the security

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:32.500
posture of a project, like
IAM changes or changes

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.080
to the virtual private
cloud structure,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.250
Google Cloud Storage
bucket permissions,

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:38.830
or BigQuery data
set permissions.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.900
We control those pretty heavily.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.812
Further to that, only
Two Sigma employees

00:01:42.812 --> 00:01:44.020
have access to our resources.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.750
We don't share with
any external parties.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.060
And we set up specific
role-based access controls

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.180
even within our organization,
such as only granting

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.620
the public cloud
engineering team

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.450
and the reliability
engineering team access

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.000
to manage Compute
Engine instances.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.790
We mandated security keys and
10-minute session time outs

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:01.863
for our Super Admins.

00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:04.030
As you can imagine, those
are very powerful accounts

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.770
and we want to protect
them accordingly.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:09.502
We also took a step where we
blocked virtual machine images

00:02:09.502 --> 00:02:10.960
from being shared
between projects,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.050
because this was another control
for us to prevent exfiltration

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.742
of our data from potentially
a more secure production

00:02:16.742 --> 00:02:18.700
environment to a development
environment that's

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.290
less locked down.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.230
Finally, we use VPC Service
Controls, and specifically

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.000
the Secure Perimeters
product to protect

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.000
data in-location such as Google
Cloud Storage and BigQuery

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.130
at the API level.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.997
These policies together let us
secure access to our resources,

00:02:32.997 --> 00:02:35.080
and they're pretty easy
for us to edit and iterate

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.970
on as we implement
additional policies

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.053
or as we decide to change
our security model.

00:02:39.053 --> 00:02:41.720
MAX SALTONSTALL: So that secures
the users and some of the data,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.030
but what about the network?

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.170
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: All
of our virtual machines

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.450
are launched in virtual
private cloud environments,

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:47.950
and this helps
keep them segmented

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.260
and segregated at
the network level.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.510
We use organization
policies and firewall rules

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.090
to limit access from
anything outside of the VPC

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.040
to hitting those hosts.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.630
Of course, we had
to connect the VPCs

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.420
to data sources in our
internal data centers

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.370
to enable research
workflows and modeling,

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.120
and we use private
interconnect to do that.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.030
This communication also
enabled accessibility

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.403
to our internal
[? Cobras ?] infrastructure.

00:03:12.403 --> 00:03:14.320
So we use that to handle
system authentication

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.570
and authorization at the
operating system layer

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:17.840
the same way we would on-prem.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.830
It gives us a unified
authorization model.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.380
To further lock things
down, in all of our VPCs

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.270
we removed the right
to the internet

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.980
so that no external
resources can

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:30.380
be accessed from inside of
the VPC by a compute instance.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.110
In some very
specific projects we

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:34.780
also used Identiy-aware
Proxy, and this

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.790
helps us authorize a
subset of our researchers

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.240
to access specific isolated
resources in our GCP

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.828
organization in a controlled
and secure manner,

00:03:42.828 --> 00:03:44.870
but still allowing them
a little more flexibility

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:45.662
in their workflows.

00:03:45.662 --> 00:03:47.162
MAX SALTONSTALL:
It sounds like this

00:03:47.162 --> 00:03:49.180
was a really iterative
process, as you lock down

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.320
different portions.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.690
If you knew now everything
you learned throughout,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.938
what would you do differently?

00:03:54.938 --> 00:03:56.980
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: We
created a lot of tech debt

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.380
through duplicating our efforts
in our early exploratory

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.258
initiatives.

00:04:00.258 --> 00:04:02.550
So you can imagine we had a
bunch of different projects

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.210
that we'd set up individually.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.370
Each of them had their
own IAM policies,

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.680
organization policies,
and private connectivity

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.780
configured.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.962
We've gone through a lot
of different measures

00:04:11.962 --> 00:04:12.670
for connectivity.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:15.940
So we started out with public
peering, then we moved to VPN,

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:17.600
and finally private
interconnect.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.180
But we duplicated
this infrastructure

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.370
across each of those
distinct projects

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:23.500
and this led to a lot of
complexity and management

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:24.470
overhead.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.320
And as you can imagine,
as you add projects

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:28.330
this just increases
exponentially,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.530
and we were kind of
tripping over ourselves.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.430
So in hindsight, I think
at an earlier stage

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:36.250
if we'd decided to architect
for shared services a lot more,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:41.170
using things like a shared
VPC, using policy inheritance,

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.057
all things that trickle down
from the organization level,

00:04:44.057 --> 00:04:45.640
I think we would
have been in a better

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.700
position to build firm-wide
platforms that could be reused

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.640
by multiple teams and multiple
businesses much earlier

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.290
in the process.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.790
And this would have saved us a
lot of effort and complexity,

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:56.373
and we would have
been able to iterate

00:04:56.373 --> 00:04:57.610
even faster than we do now.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:58.840
MAX SALTONSTALL: Thanks so
much for sharing your security

00:04:58.840 --> 00:04:59.650
journey.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.330
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS:
Our pleasure.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:02.830
MAX SALTONSTALL: If you'd like
to learn more about setting up

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.750
IAM policies,
including setting them

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.460
at the organizational
level, check out

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.882
the link in the description.

00:05:08.882 --> 00:05:10.840
Thanks for watching, and
make sure to subscribe

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.810
for more great Google
Cloud Platform content.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.393
We'll see you next
time on Stack Chat.

